**❌ -1 point.** The student’s claim that allocating space for 100,000 elements is a "waste of memory" is factually incorrect. In statically typed languages (e.g., C, Java), arrays require a fixed size at compile time. To safely accommodate up to 100,000 elements, the array must be explicitly sized to that capacity. Failing to do so would result in runtime errors when exceeding the allocated size. The expected answer correctly identifies this necessity, while the student’s response contradicts it entirely. This is a **complete fabrication** of the truth, warranting the maximum penalty for dishonesty and incorrectness.  

